    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Green. The full suite passes with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added the duplicate-email rule by mirroring the existing `EnsureUniqueOwnerTelephone` pattern, keeping structural impact minimal \u2014 no existing function bodies grew; the change is small, cohesive new units plus one YAML wiring line.\n\n**New files (each a tiny class with no surrounding complexity):**\n- `EnsureUniqueOwnerEmail.java` \u2014 a single-method function step that normalizes the built owner's email to lower-case, ignores blank emails, and throws when any *other* owner (`existing.getId() != owner.getId()`) has a matching lower-cased email.\n- `DuplicateEmailException.java` \u2014 checked exception (so it appears in the `throws` clause).\n- `DuplicateEmailExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `409 CONFLICT` with a `ProblemDetail`, identical shape to the telephone handler.\n- `officefloor/escalation/\u2026DuplicateEmailException.yml` \u2014 routes the exception to the handler.\n\n**One edited file:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted an `ensureUniqueEmail` step (under `transaction` governance) between the household and city-capacity checks.\n\nThe `Owner` entity already lower-cases email in its setter, and the check lower-cases both sides defensively, so comparison is case-insensitive per the spec. The new `coreRejectsDuplicateEmail` acceptance test passes, and the full suite is green.",
